# Gotchas

The demos elsewhere in these docs run under ideal conditions: a known photo, a fixed-size box, nothing animating. Real apps are messier. This page collects the problems that tend to appear only once a cropper ships, each with a fix you can try in place.

## Uploads that can't be read

**Symptom:** the customer picks a photo, and by the time the cropper reads it, the browser can't: the app revoked the object URL, or a phone evicted the file from memory under pressure. With react-advanced-cropper (and svelte-advanced-cropper before 0.1.2), the load then never finishes: neither `onReady` nor `onError` fires, and the spinner spins forever.

**Cause:** by default the cropper reads the photo's EXIF orientation first (`checkOrientation` is `true`). For a `blob:` URL, the kind `URL.createObjectURL()` gives you for an upload, the [advanced-cropper](https://github.com/advanced-cropper/advanced-cropper) core reads the blob with a request that has no error handler, so the failure goes unnoticed.

**Fix:** from version 0.1.2, svelte-advanced-cropper checks that a blob URL is still readable and reports `onError` when it isn't, whatever `checkOrientation` is set to. What's left for you is to handle `onError`, so the customer sees a message and can pick another photo:

```svelte
<Cropper
	src={uploadedUrl}
	onReady={() => (status = 'ready')}
	onError={() => (status = 'error')}
/>
```

`checkOrientation={false}` is still a reasonable choice for uploads, just no longer required for this. Current versions of Chrome, Firefox and Safari apply a photo's EXIF orientation themselves when they display or draw it, so the cropper's own read mostly duplicates the browser's work. Turning it off also saves memory: the read loads the whole file, and for a rotated photo the cropper keeps a second copy while it's shown. On react-advanced-cropper it remains the workaround for the hang.

One difference to know before you turn it off: with `checkOrientation` on, the cropper reports the photo's rotation as its own. `getImage()` gives the size the file was stored at, and `getTransforms()` includes the rotation, so a portrait iPhone photo reports `rotate: 90`. With it off, the browser has already turned the photo upright: the size is the upright size and the rotation is `0`. The crop coordinates are the same either way. This matters if you save the transforms and replay them somewhere else, such as a server that also turns photos upright, which would then turn them twice.

"Load a broken upload" hands the cropper a revoked blob URL. Try it with both settings:

<Example source={failedLoadSource} title="FailedLoadExample">
	<FailedLoadExample />
</Example>

## iPhone photos (HEIC)

**Symptom:** a photo from an iPhone uploads fine but never shows. On Chrome, Firefox and Edge the cropper stays empty, and only `onError` tells you anything happened.

**Cause:** iPhones save photos as HEIC by default. Safari decodes HEIC, but Chrome, Firefox and Edge don't, mostly because of the patent licensing around the HEVC codec inside it. The cropper hands the file to the browser, the browser can't decode it, and the cropper reports `onError`.

**Fix:** convert HEIC to JPEG in the browser before cropping, but only when the browser can't decode it itself. [heic-to](https://github.com/hoppergee/heic-to) does the conversion with a current build of libheif, in a web worker. The helper below checks the file's first bytes (picked files don't always carry a name or MIME type), tries the browser's own decoder first so Safari users download nothing extra, and only then loads heic-to:

<CodeBlock html={croppablePhotoSource.html} code={croppablePhotoSource.code} />

Use the URL it returns as the cropper's `src`, show a message while it works, and keep handling `onError` for photos that still can't be opened. Try it with an iPhone photo:

<Example source={heicUploadSource} title="HeicUploadExample">
	<HeicUploadExample />
</Example>

svelte-advanced-cropper doesn't convert HEIC itself. heic-to is about 780 KB compressed and LGPL-licensed, which would be a heavy cost for every app using the cropper; with the dynamic import above, only someone who uploads a HEIC photo in a browser that can't decode it downloads it. Avoid `heic2any`, which many guides still recommend: it hasn't been updated since 2023 and fails on the 24 MP photos recent iPhones take by default.

## Picked photos that stop loading later

**Symptom:** a photo the customer picked a few minutes ago won't load when the cropper finally opens, and `onError` fires, though the same photo showed fine as a thumbnail right after it was picked. It happens mostly on Android, and is hard to reproduce on purpose.

**Cause:** a `File` from `<input type="file">` is a reference to the photo, not a copy of it. On Android the reference is usually a handle onto a document that belongs to another app, and the permission to read it can run out while your page is still open. On a desktop, the same happens if the file is changed or deleted after it was picked. From then on the browser refuses every read: `file.arrayBuffer()` throws a `NotReadableError`, and an object URL made from the file stops loading. The cropper only ever sees that URL, so reporting `onError` is all it can do.

**Fix:** copy the bytes as soon as the photo is picked, while reading still works, and make the object URL from the copy:

```ts
async function pickedPhotoUrl(file: File): Promise<string> {
	// A copy this page holds stays readable for as long as the tab is open.
	const copy = new Blob([await file.arrayBuffer()], { type: file.type });

	return URL.createObjectURL(copy);
}
```

The copy takes as much memory as the file until you revoke its URL, so revoke it once the photo is replaced. The `croppablePhotoUrl` helper in the previous section already copies: a photo it converts from HEIC is a new file by construction, and any other photo is copied as above.

## Exports that fail on phones

**Symptom:** exporting works on your desktop, but on a phone the upload sometimes arrives empty or never happens, and nothing is logged. Or the exported JPEG has black corners where the photo was transparent.

**Cause:**

- `getCanvas()` exports at the crop's full resolution unless you limit it, and a crop from a 24 MP phone photo can be 6000 px wide. That can exceed iOS Safari's canvas limit (about 16.7 million pixels) or the memory the tab has left.
- When it does, `canvas.toBlob()` calls back with `null` instead of throwing. Code that returns early on `null`, as many upload examples do, then fails silently.
- JPEG has no transparency, so transparent areas (a PNG's, or a crop that reaches past the photo's edges) are encoded as black.

**Fix:** always [limit the export's size](/docs/guides/recipes#resize-the-result), treat `null` as a failure (try again smaller, then tell the user), and pass `fillColor` when encoding a JPEG:

<CodeBlock html={exportCropSource.html} code={exportCropSource.code} />

`getCanvas()` also returns the same canvas element every time, drawing each export into it. Turn it into a `Blob` rather than keeping it (`toBlob()` takes its snapshot when you call it), then shrink it as the helper does: otherwise its pixels stay in memory until the next export, 16 MB for 2048 × 2048.

## Keeping your opening view after a resize

**Symptom:** the cropper opens showing the whole photo, exactly as your `defaultVisibleArea` asks. After the window gets wider, the photo is suddenly zoomed in and no longer fits.

**Cause:** `defaultVisibleArea` only applies when the cropper resets, that is, when a new image loads or you call `reset()`. On a window resize the cropper calls `refresh()`, which measures the new boundary but keeps the current zoom, changing it only as far as the restrictions require. Shrinking usually forces a re-fit; growing doesn't. A second, quieter problem: the cropper listens to the _window_. If only its container changes size (a sidebar opens, a panel is dragged wider), it doesn't notice at all.

**Fix:** watch the container yourself and apply the opening view again after refreshing:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { Cropper, type CropperRef, type CropperState } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';

	let { src }: { src: string } = $props();

	let cropper: CropperRef | undefined = $state();
	let width = $state(0);
	let height = $state(0);

	function fitWholeImage(state: CropperState) {
		// The same function you pass as defaultVisibleArea.
	}

	$effect(() => {
		void width;
		void height;
		if (cropper) {
			void refit(cropper);
		}
	});

	async function refit(target: CropperRef) {
		await target.refresh();
		const state = target.getState();
		if (state) {
			target.setVisibleArea(fitWholeImage(state), { transitions: false });
		}
	}
</script>

<div class="cropper-box" bind:clientWidth={width} bind:clientHeight={height}>
	<Cropper bind:this={cropper} {src} defaultVisibleArea={fitWholeImage} />
</div>
```

`bind:clientWidth` and `bind:clientHeight` follow the container through any change, window resizes included. The crop the user made is never touched: only the visible area is set. Calling ref methods from an effect is safe; they don't make the effect depend on the cropper's state.

You may see another fix in the wild: save `getCoordinates()`, `await reset()`, then `setCoordinates()` with the saved crop. It also re-applies the opening view, but `reset()` throws away rotation, flips and zoom as well, and the default crop flashes for a frame. Prefer `refresh()` plus `setVisibleArea()`.

Drag the width slider with each option selected:

<Example source={refitSource} title="RefitExample">
	<RefitExample />
</Example>

## Croppers in dialogs and scaled containers

Crop UIs often live in a dialog, and dialogs often scale in: from 95% to full size is a common entrance. The cropper is usually ready, and measures itself, while that animation is still running.

A cropper that measured its _on-screen_ size would size its crop area to the shrunken dialog and stay wrong after the animation ended. react-advanced-cropper measures that way (with `getBoundingClientRect()`), which is why React codebases sometimes carry a "never animate the dialog's scale" rule. svelte-advanced-cropper (from version 0.1.1) measures its _layout_ size instead, which CSS transforms don't affect, so a scaling dialog needs no special care. Open the dialog below: it scales in from 90%, and the cropper measures its true size anyway.

<Example source={dialogSource} title="DialogExample">
	<DialogExample />
</Example>

If you replace the boundary's size algorithm with one that measures the on-screen size, measure again once the dialog has opened, for example from Svelte's `introend` event or your dialog library's "opened" callback:

```svelte
<div class="dialog" in:scale onintroend={() => cropper?.refresh()}>
	<Cropper bind:this={cropper} {src} boundaryProps={{ sizeAlgorithm: myOnScreenMeasure }} />
</div>
```

### Dragging inside a permanently scaled container

A cropper can also live inside an element that _stays_ scaled, such as a preview pane shrunk with `transform: scale(0.75)`. Pointer events report screen pixels, while the cropper works in the container's own, unscaled pixels. A cropper that used the pointer's distances as they are would move the stencil by only 75% of the drag, so it would trail the cursor; react-advanced-cropper behaves that way, and so did svelte-advanced-cropper before 0.1.3. From 0.1.3, drags, pans, pinches and wheel zooms are converted through the container's scale, so they follow the pointer at any scale.

Drag the stencil at each scale. The reading below the demo compares how far it moved with how far the pointer did:

<Example source={scaledContainerSource} title="ScaledContainerExample">
	<ScaledContainerExample />
</Example>

Only scaling is accounted for. A cropper inside a rotated or skewed container still gets confused, so keep those transforms off its ancestors.

## Give the cropper a definite height

**Symptom:** the cropper spills out of its dialog or panel, or the page scrolls even though the container has a `max-height`.

**Cause:** the cropper fills its container's height (`max-height: 100%`), but a percentage height only works against a parent with a _definite_ height. Without one, the cropper sizes itself to the photo instead: in a 600px-wide box, a 4:3 photo makes it 450px tall, even inside a parent limited to `max-height: 300px`.

**Fix:** give the parent a real `height`, not only a `max-height`. In a flex column, the cropper's flex item also needs `min-height: 0`, and the column itself needs a definite height:

```css
.dialog {
	display: flex;
	flex-direction: column;
	height: 90vh; /* not max-height */
}

.dialog-body {
	flex: 1;
	min-height: 0; /* lets the flex item shrink below its content */
}

.dialog-body :global(.advanced-cropper) {
	height: 100%;
}
```
